th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/divergence-to-infinity.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/divergence-to-infinity.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\lim_{x\\to x_0} f(x) = +\\infty$, what is required after choosing any $M\\in\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["There exists $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $|x-x_0| = 0$ implies $f(x) \\gt M$.","There exists $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $f(x) \\gt M$.","There exists $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $f(x) \\lt M$.","For every $\\delta \\gt 0$, $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $f(x) \\gt M$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fbecad795dd1df7aa64ce36f","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which example has $\\lim_{x\\to 0} f(x) = +\\infty$?","options":["$f(x) = 1/|x|$","$f(x) = x$","$f(x) = -1/|x|$","$f(x) = 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c4bd154e37487e8c03769181","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which one-sided statement holds for $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$ near $0$?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}\\frac{1}{x} = +\\infty$","$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = -\\infty$","$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}\\frac{1}{x} = -\\infty$","$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x} = 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9b45b0c349163431e83fee4e","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$ on the left of $0$, which condition ensures $f(x) \\lt -100$?","options":["$0 \\lt x \\lt 1/100$","$-100 \\lt x \\lt 0$","$-1/100 \\lt x \\lt 0$","$-1/100 \\lt x \\lt 1/100$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d607344f4d2ae47467bcdd9d","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_divergence_to_infinity_new_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=+\\infty$ for a real $x_0$, what kind of asymptote is the line $x=x_0$?","options":["vertical asymptote","horizontal asymptote","tangent line","point of continuity"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8b575880421a11d48545fe1d","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_divergence_to_infinity_new_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f$ diverges to $+\\infty$ at $x_0$, does it have a finite limit there?","options":["No; there is no real limit value","Yes; the limit is $+\\infty$, a real number","No; but its one-sided limits are finite","Yes; the limit is the largest value of $f$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"65e3b1ed67d545b3e8f71d5e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition of $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=+\\infty$ use $0<|x-x_0|<\\delta$ rather than $|x-x_0|<\\delta$?","options":["It requires $f$ to be defined only at $x_0$.","It forces the limit to equal the value $f(x_0)$.","It looks at $x$ near $x_0$ but excludes $x_0$ itself.","It restricts $x$ to one side of $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4b503a02b26621800af34340","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_divergence_to_infinity_41df806d_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ for $x\\ne0$. Which statement describes the two-sided behaviour of $f$ as $x\\to0$?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1}{x}=+\\infty$, because $|1/x|$ grows without bound.","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1}{x}=-\\infty$, because the left side dominates.","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1}{x}=0$, because the two sides cancel.","No two-sided limit $\\pm\\infty$: the one-sided limits differ."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fc8bbe8548e2b14a371500b4","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_divergence_to_infinity_new_005_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f$ be defined on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. Compare (I) $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=+\\infty$ and (II) $f$ is unbounded above on every punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. Which is correct?","options":["(I) implies (II), but (II) does not imply (I).","(I) and (II) are equivalent.","(II) implies (I), but (I) does not imply (II).","Neither of (I) and (II) implies the other."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6a0f44912fb749a39ec93607","time_limit":40},{"id":"expansion_pack_017_divergence_to_infinity_new_006_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x)=1/|x|$, which condition on $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$ is equivalent to \"$x$ is in the domain of $f$ and $f(x)>100$\"?","options":["$0<|x|<100$","$|x|<1/100$","$0<|x|<1/100$","$1/100<|x|<100$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4fa2a0639486008d5ae3541","time_limit":25}]}}